# AUTHORITATIVE CONSOLIDATED FULL SUITE & PROGRAMME CLOSURE

Canonical document:
`docs/sprints/authoritative-consolidated-full-suite-and-closure-1.md`.
This rule is the durable mirror. Where they differ, the document governs.
Pinned by `tests/Feature/Cicd/AuthoritativeConsolidatedFullSuiteClosureContractTest.php`.

## CLOSE-R01 — What "the stabilization programme is closed" means

The programme is closed only when ALL of the following hold:

- `FINAL-STABILIZATION-RESIDUAL-AUDIT-1` is GO;
- exactly one explicitly-authorised consolidated Full Suite has PASSED;
- the exact passed candidate tree is the merged tree AND the deployed tree;
- final production verification passes;
- no blocking residual remains;
- the final authority is GO-tagged.

Anything less is not closure. In particular a green *required-gate* set is not
closure, because the required gates deliberately do not run the whole suite.

## CLOSE-R02 — One-run Full Suite rule

A failed final authoritative Full Suite is **NOT** automatically rerun.

```
FULL SUITE FAIL
  -> freeze the exact failure set
  -> root-cause with TARGETED tests only
  -> corrective sprint
  -> a new complete-suite execution requires NEW explicit user authorisation
```

Forbidden as a route to closure: rerunning the job, "rerun failed jobs",
re-dispatching after an edit, allowlisting a failure, marking a real failure
flaky without reproducing it, or skipping/quarantining a failing test.

A run that was cancelled, timed out, lost its runner, or produced partial
output is **not** a PASS and **not** a consumed authorisation — but restarting
it is still a new execution and still needs authorisation.

## CLOSE-R03 — Tree identity, not commit identity

A squash merge legitimately produces a different commit SHA than the tested
candidate. That is acceptable **only** when the git *tree* is identical:

```
FULL_SUITE_PASSED_CANDIDATE_TREE == RUNTIME_MERGE_TREE == DEPLOYED_TREE
```

If the trees differ, the Full Suite did not test what was deployed, and the
result is NO-GO. Verify with `git rev-parse <sha>^{tree}` on each side —
never infer identity from a passing pipeline.

After a PASS and before merge/deploy the candidate is **immutable**: no
runtime, test, config, migration, CI, rules or governance-semantic change.
Any such change invalidates the evidence.

## CLOSE-R04 — What closure does NOT claim

Closure of the stabilization programme does **not** mean any separately
authorised external integration or deferred roadmap item is complete. These
retain their audited classifications and remain OFF:

- WhatsApp / Meta prescription delivery — `BLOCKED_EXTERNAL` (awaits credentials);
- SATUSEHAT external submission and production activation — `BLOCKED_EXTERNAL`
  (its GO tag is deliberately ABSENT; integration stays DISABLED);
- object-storage production cutover — `DEFERRED` (production authority remains
  the private local disk);
- the historical dangling storage references — `ACCEPTED_RISK`, classified and
  deliberately **not** deleted.

Never describe closure as "everything finished".

## CLOSE-R05 — Retiring the temporary Full-Suite policy is a separate act

The `GLOBAL TEMPORARY FULL-SUITE POLICY` (rule `107`) must **not** be flipped to
`RETIRED` inside the closure candidate itself, for two independent reasons:

1. **Truth.** Policy s10 retires it only once closure is *finished*; at candidate
   freeze the Full Suite has not yet run.
2. **Safety.** `RETIRED` re-authorises the automatic `push`-to-base path, so
   merging the closure PR would immediately fire a SECOND, unauthorised Full
   Suite — breaking CLOSE-R02 mechanically.

Retirement is therefore a distinct, explicitly-authorised post-closure
governance act. On retirement, mark the policy document and rule `107`
`RETIRED after consolidated Full Suite closure` and **keep both** as historical
evidence.

## CLOSE-R06 — A dispatched Full Suite must be proven to have executed

The Full Suite *step* is guarded by `run_critical_tests != 'false'`. A change
set of Markdown alone resolves to `docs_only`, which sets that output to
`false`: the job then reports success having executed **no tests**.

Before trusting any authoritative run, confirm both:

- the classifier resolved the change set to a profile with
  `run_critical_tests=true` (updating `.sprint/current.yml` guarantees this —
  it matches no classifier pattern and resolves to `unknown_high_risk`); and
- the run reports a real `Tests:` line with a non-zero test count.

A skipped Full Suite is a false green and does **not** consume the
authorisation.
